 absolute value of d_4 is at most 630*27 +168*27 +28*27= (630 +168 +28)*27=826*27=22302, which is way larger than 27. So even d_4 would be enormous.\n\nBut wait, this seems to suggest that multiple coefficients would be large, but our earlier analysis shows that even d_3 cannot be bounded by 27. Therefore, the conclusion holds.\n\nBut maybe I should present this as a formal proof.\n\nAssume for contradiction that all coefficients of P(x) have absolute value at most 27. Then, as shown above, we express a, b, c in terms of deltas and previous variables, leading to the coefficient d_3 being a combination that necessarily exceeds 27 in absolute value, contradicting the assumption. Therefore, at least one coefficient must have absolute value greater than 27.\n\nTherefore, the proof is complete.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that a 10th-degree polynomial \\( P(x) \\) with integer coefficients, having 1 as a root at least eight times, must have at least one coefficient with absolute value greater than 27, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1: Factorize the Polynomial**  \nSince 1 is a root with multiplicity at least 8, \\( P(x) \\) can be written as:\n\\[\nP(x) = (x - 1)^8 \\cdot Q(x),\n\\]\nwhere \\( Q(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \\) is a quadratic polynomial with integer coefficients (\\( a, b, c \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\), \\( a \\neq 0 \\)).\n\n**Step 2: Expand \\( (x - 1)^8 \\)**  \nUsing the binomial theorem:\n\\[\n(x - 1)^8 = \\sum_{k=0}^8 \\binom{8}{k} (-1)^k x^{8-k} = x^8 - 8x^7 + 28x^6 - 56x^5 + 70x^4 - 56x^3 + 28x^2 - 8x + 1.\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Multiply by \\( Q(x) \\)**  \nThe coefficients of \\( P(x) \\) are derived by convolving the coefficients of \\( (x - 1)^8 \\) with \\( Q(x) \\). For example:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nd_{10} &= a, \\\\\nd_9 &= -8a + b, \\\\\nd_8 &= 28a - 8b + c, \\\\\nd_7 &= -56a + 28b - 8c, \\\\\n&\\vdots \\\\\nd_0 &= c.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n**Step 4: Assume All Coefficients Are Bounded**  \nSuppose, for contradiction, that all coefficients \\( |d_k| \\leq 27 \\). This implies:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n|c| &\\leq 27, \\\\\n|b - 8c| &\\leq 27, \\\\\n|a - 8b + 28c| &\\leq 27, \\\\\n&\\vdots \\\\\n|-56a + 70b - 56c| &\\leq 27.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n**Step 5: Derive a Contradiction**  \nExpress \\( b \\) and \\( a \\) in terms of \\( c \\):\n\\[\nb = 8c + \\delta_1 \\quad (|\\delta_1| \\leq 27), \\quad a = 36c + 8\\delta_1 + \\delta_2 \\quad (|\\delta_2| \\leq 27).\n\\]\nSubstitute \\( a, b, c \\) into the coefficient \\( d_3 \\):\n\\[\nd_3 = -8a + 28b - 56c = -120c - 36\\delta_1 - 8\\delta_2.\n\\]\nEven if \\( c = 0 \\), \\( d_3 = -36\\delta_1 - 8\\delta_2 \\). Given \\( |\\delta_1|, |\\delta_2| \\leq 27 \\), the maximum possible absolute value is:\n\\[\n|d_3| \\leq 36 \\cdot 27 + 8 \\cdot 27 = 1188,\n\\]\nwhich far exceeds 27. This contradiction implies our initial assumption is false.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nTherefore, at least one coefficient of \\( P(x) \\) must have an absolute value greater than 27. This completes the proof.", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem\nA polynomial of degree 10 with integer coefficients has 1 as a root of multiplicity at least eight. Prove that there exists a coefficient whose absolute value is greater than 27.\n\n1. Given that the polynomial has 1 as a root of multiplicity at least eight, it can be written in the form:\n   \\[\n   f(x) = (x-1)^8(a x^2 + b x + c)\n   \\]\n   where \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are real numbers, and \\(a \\neq 0\\).\n\n2. Expanding \\( f(x) \\) using the binomial theorem and polynomial multiplication, we obtain:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   f(x) &= (x-1)^8 \\cdot (a x^2 + b x + c) \\\\\n   &= a x^{10} + (b - 8a) x^9 + (c - 8b + 28a) x^8 + (-8c + 28b - 56a) x^7 \\\\\n   &\\quad + (28c - 56b + 70a) x^6 + (-56c + 70b - 56a) x^5 + (70c - 56b \\ldots)\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n3. Notice that the coefficients should be integers since \\( f(x) \\) is given to have integer coefficients. Thus, \\( a \\), \\( c \\), and \\( b - 8a \\) must be integers, meaning \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are integers.\n\n4. Assume, contrary to what we want to prove, that the absolute values of all coefficients are less than 28. Analyzing the fourth-, fifth-, and sixth-degree terms and their coefficients, we write the inequalities:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{align*}\n   |5c - 4b + 2a| &< 28 \\\\\n   |-4c + 5b - 4a| &< 28 \\\\\n   |2c - 4b + 5a| &< 28\n   \\end{align*}\n   \\]\n\n5. Since \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) are integers, any expression involving \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( c \\) resulting in an inequality less than 28 must round to at most 1. This is because the terms are linear combinations of integers.\n\n6. Considering \\( 3|3c - 3b + 2a| \\) and the difference of inequalities:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   |3c - 3b + 2a| &= |(5c - 4b + 2a) - (-4c + 5b - 4a)| \\\\\n   &< 2 + 2 = 4 \\\\\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n   This implies:\n   \\[\n   |3c - 3b + 2a| = 0\n   \\]\n   However, for integers, this means:\n   \\[\n   3c - 3b + 2a = 0\n   \\]\n\n7. Similarly, consider \\( 2 c - 3 b + 3 a \\):\n   \\[\n   3|2c - 3b + 3a| = |2c - 4b + 5a| + | -4c + 5b - 4a| \\\\\n   2c - 3b + 3a = 0\n   \\]\n\n8. The differences imply \\( c = a \\) and \\( b = \\frac{5}{3} a \\), and thus \\( b \\) is not an integer if \\(a\\) is an integer.\n\n9. Analyzing the seventh-degree coefficient and using the obtained \\(c\\) and \\(b\\):\n   \\[\n   |8a - 28 \\cdot \\frac{5}{3} a + 56a| = \\left| \\frac{52}{3} a \\right| < 28\n   \\]\n   Simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   |a| < \\frac{21}{13}\n   \\]\n   Which approximates that \\(a < 2\\). But \\(a\\) must be an integer, thus \\(a = \\pm 1\\). However, this results in \\(b\\) being non-integer, which is a contradiction.\n\n### Conclusion:\nOur initial assumption that all coefficients have absolute values less than 28 leads to a contradiction. Therefore, there must be at least one coefficient whose absolute value is greater than **27**.\n\\[\n\\boxed{27}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "num