 specific example. Suppose the circle has radius 2, and point A is at (1,0). Then, according to this result, the points M would be at (1, sqrt(4 - 1)) = (1, \u221a3) and (1, -\u221a3). Let me compute angle AMO at this point.\n\nPoint M is (1, \u221a3). Then, vectors MA and MO are:\n\nMA = A - M = (1 - 1, 0 - \u221a3) = (0, -\u221a3)\n\nMO = O - M = (-1, -\u221a3)\n\nThe angle between MA and MO can be found using the dot product:\n\ncos \u03c6 = (MA \u00b7 MO) / (|MA| |MO| )\n\nMA \u00b7 MO = (0)(-1) + (-\u221a3)(-\u221a3) = 0 + 3 = 3\n\n|MA| = sqrt(0\u00b2 + (-\u221a3)\u00b2) = \u221a3\n\n|MO| = sqrt((-1)^2 + (-\u221a3)^2) = sqrt(1 + 3) = 2\n\nSo,\n\ncos \u03c6 = 3 / (\u221a3 * 2 ) = 3 / (2\u221a3 ) = \u221a3 / 2 \u2248 0.866\n\nTherefore, \u03c6 = arccos(\u221a3 / 2 ) = 30 degrees.\n\nWait, but is this the maximum angle? Let's check another point. Suppose M is at (2,0), which is the point diametrically opposite to O in the x-direction. Wait, but O is the center. If the circle has radius 2, then (2,0) is on the circle. But point A is at (1,0). Then, angle AMO would be the angle at M between A(1,0) and O(0,0). Let's compute that.\n\nVectors MA = A - M = (1 - 2, 0 - 0) = (-1, 0)\n\nMO = O - M = (0 - 2, 0 - 0) = (-2, 0)\n\nThe angle between (-1,0) and (-2,0) is 0 degrees, since they are in the same direction. So, angle AMO here is 0 degrees. That's definitely not the maximum.\n\nWhat if M is at (0,2), the top of the circle. Then, vectors MA and MO:\n\nMA = A - M = (1 - 0, 0 - 2) = (1, -2)\n\nMO = O - M = (0 - 0, 0 - 2) = (0, -2)\n\nDot product MA \u00b7 MO = (1)(0) + (-2)(-2) = 4\n\n|MA| = sqrt(1\u00b2 + (-2)^2 ) = sqrt(5)\n\n|MO| = 2\n\ncos \u03c6 = 4 / (sqrt(5)*2 ) = 2 / sqrt(5 ) \u2248 0.894\n\nThus, \u03c6 \u2248 arccos(0.894) \u2248 26.6 degrees, which is less than 30 degrees. So, angle at (0,2) is smaller.\n\nWait, so according to this, the maximum angle occurs at points directly above and below A, giving 30 degrees, which is more than the other tested points.\n\