ertices at A(0,0), D(0, x), K(?, ?), and M(y, 0). Wait, but since it's a rectangle, the sides AD and AM are adjacent sides. So from A(0,0), moving up to D(0,x), and right to M(y,0). Then the fourth vertex K must be at (y, x), but since the rectangle is ADKM, the sides DK and MK must be parallel to AM and AD, respectively.\n\nWait, no. Let me think again. If ADKM is a rectangle, then sides AD and AM are adjacent. So starting at A(0,0), moving along AB (upwards) to D(0, x), then moving horizontally to K, and then moving down to M(y, 0), and back to A(0,0). Wait, but in that case, the horizontal side from D(0,x) should have the same length as AM = y. So K would be at (y, x). But then K has coordinates (y, x). However, K is supposed to lie on BC. So point K(y, x) must satisfy the equation of BC.\n\nThe equation of BC is from (0,5) to (12,0). Let's confirm that. The slope is (0 - 5)/(12 - 0) = -5/12. So the equation is y = (-5/12)x + 5. So any point on BC must satisfy this equation.\n\nBut point K is (y, x). Wait, hold on. Wait, if K is at (y, x), then substituting into the equation of BC, we get x = (-5/12)y + 5. Therefore, x = (-5/12)y + 5. But we also have the area of the rectangle as x * y = 40/3. So we can set up the equations:\n\n1. x = (-5/12)y + 5\n\n2. x * y = 40/3\n\nSo substitute equation 1 into equation 2:\n\n[(-5/12)y + 5] * y = 40/3\n\nMultiply through:\n\n(-5/12)y\u00b2 + 5y = 40/3\n\nMultiply both sides by 12 to eliminate denominators:\n\n-5y\u00b2 + 60y = 160\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n-5y\u00b2 + 60y - 160 = 0\n\nMultiply both sides by -1:\n\n5y\u00b2 - 60y + 160 = 0\n\nDivide all terms by 5:\n\ny\u00b2 - 12y + 32 = 0\n\nFactor this quadratic equation:\n\nLooking for two numbers that multiply to 32 and add to 12. 8 and 4: 8*4=32, 8+4=12.\n\nSo, (y - 8)(y - 4) = 0\n\nThus, y = 8 or y = 4\n\nBut since AC is 12 units long, and AM = y is along AC, y must be between 0 and 12. So both 8 and 4 are valid. Then x is calculated from equation 1.\n\nIf y = 