f four people must not answer at least one question correctly.\n\n2. **Analysis**:\n   - Each question must be answered correctly by at least six people. This ensures that any group of five people will include at least one person who answered correctly, satisfying the first condition.\n   - For the second condition, each group of four people must not answer at least one question. This means each four-person group must be the complement (people who answered incorrectly) of at least one question.\n\n3. **Combinatorial Reasoning**:\n   - Each question's complement (people who answered incorrectly) must be a four-person group.\n   - To cover all possible four-person groups, each four-person group must be the complement of at least one question.\n   - The number of four-person groups in ten people is given by the combination \\( \\binom{10}{4} \\).\n\n4. **Calculation**:\n   - The number of four-person groups is \\( \\binom{10}{4} = 210 \\).\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   - Each four-person group must be the complement of a question, leading to the minimum number of questions being 210.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{210}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "Given the problem, we are asked to find the minimum number of questions \\( q \\) required such that any group of ten people can meet the specified conditions:\n1. Any five people together have answered all questions.\n2. Any four people together have not answered all the questions.\n\nTo approach this problem, let's follow these steps:\n\n1. **Consider the maximum number of participants who might not answer a specific question:**\n    By the problem's definition, if any five people together have answered all questions, then at most four people can fail to answer a particular question. This implies that for each question, there is at least one person in every group of five who can answer it. Therefore, each question can be left unanswered by at most four people.\n\n2. **Determine the total number of combinations for groups of four:**\n    W