hes can deactivate all lamps i.e., extinguish the lamps in the main layer.\n\n6. **Simulating the Deactivation Process**:\n   - **Petya's Switches**: Let $\\mathcal{P}$ be the set of switches on the red face used by Petya, and $\\mathcal{Q}$ be the set of switches used by Petya on blue or green faces.\n   - Vasya: Apply $\\mathcal{P}$ and $\\mathcal{Q}$ to obtain the intermediate lamp states post-Petya's operations.\n\n7. **Continuing the Proof**:\n   - Post Petya\u2019s operations, the main layer still shows $d$ lamps on. By pressing the switches in $\\mathcal{V}$, all lamps in the main layer will be turned off.\n   - For each of the remaining layers, we create corresponding sets analogous to $\\mathcal{Q}$ and replicate the switch application process on these individual layers.\n\n8. **Completeness of the Process**:\n   - Applying all these operations (analogous to $\\mathcal{Q}$ for each layer), Vasya can ensure that all lamps are off. Thus by judiciously toggling the switches exactly related to $\\frac{k}{100}$ lamps in the crucial layers identified.\n\n### Conclusion:\n    Hence, Vasya can use at most $\\frac{k}{100}$ switches on the red face to ensure that no lamps are on. \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Proved}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "\nBy the end of the semester, Vasily Petrov had the following grades in his math journal: \\(4, 1, 2, 5, 2\\). Before finalizing the semester grade, the math teacher told Vasily:\n\n- Vasily, you can choose the method for determining your semester grade. I propose two options. Method A: the arithmetic mean of your current grades, rounded to the nearest whole number. Method B: the median of your current grades.\n\nThe best method for Vasily is the one that gives him the highest possible semester grade.\n\na) Which method is best for Vasily?\n\nb) Then the teacher added:\n\n- Keep in mind, Vasily, that if you manage to choose the best method for yourself, I