)^2 \\right) \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (\\sqrt{b_i})^2 \\right).\n\\]\n\nSimplifying both sides, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i \\right)^2 \\leq \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\frac{a_i^2}{b_i} \\right) \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} b_i \\right).\n\\]\n\nDividing both sides by \\(\\sum_{i=1}^{n} b_i\\), which is positive since all \\(b_i\\) are positive, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i \\right)^2}{\\sum_{i=1}^{n} b_i} \\leq \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\frac{a_i^2}{b_i}.\n\\]\n\nThis rearranges to the desired inequality:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a_{1}^{2}}{b_{1}} + \\ldots + \\frac{a_{n}^{2}}{b_{n}} \\geqslant \\frac{\\left(a_{1} + \\ldots + a_{n}\\right)^{2}}{b_{1} + \\ldots + b_{n}}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the inequality is proven using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{a_{1}^{2}}{b_{1}} + \\ldots + \\frac{a_{n}^{2}}{b_{n}} \\geqslant \\frac{\\left(a_{1} + \\ldots + a_{n}\\right)^{2}}{b_{1} + \\ldots + b_{n}}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. We aim to prove the inequality:\n\\[\n\\frac{a_{1}^{2}}{b_{1}} + \\frac{a_{2}^{2}}{b_{2}} + \\cdots + \\frac{a_{n}^{2}}{b_{n}} \\geq \\frac{\\left(a_{1} + a_{2} + \\cdots + a_{n}\\right)^{2}}{b_{1} + b_{2} + \\cdots + b_{n}}\n\\]\n\n2. Notice that this inequality resembles the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, which states that for any sequences of real numbers \\(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n\\) and \\(y_1, y_2, \\ldots, y_n\\), we have:\n\\[\n(x_1^2 + x_2^2 + \\cdots + x_n^2) (y_1^2 + y_2^2 + \\cdots + y_n^2) \\geq \\left( x_1 y_1 + x_2 y_2 + \\cdots + x_n y_n \\right)^2\n\\]\n\n3. Apply the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality with the sequences \\(x_i = \\frac{a_i}{\\sqrt{b_i}}\\) and \\(y_i = \\sqrt{b_i}\\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left( \\frac{a_i}{\\sqrt{b_i}} \\right)^2 \\right) \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left( \\sqrt{b_i} \\right)^2 \\right) \\geq \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\left( \\frac{a_i}{\\sqrt{b_i}} \\cdot \\sqrt{b_i} \\right) \\right)^2\n\\]\n\n4. Simplify both sides of the inequality:\n\\[\n\\left( \\sum_{i=1}^{n