be the extra66.666... comes from the fact that the all-same-residue combinations are not evenly distributed across the three sums, but in reality, they are all contributing to sum\u22610, hence the imbalance.\n\nYes, that's it. The all-same-residue combinations all contribute to sum\u22610 mod3, which is why sum\u22610 has more combinations than the others. If all combinations were either one-from-each-residue or two-from-one-and-one-from-another, then the sums would be evenly distributed. However, the presence of the all-same-residue combinations (which only contribute to sum\u22610) tips the balance.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is indeed1,485,100, as calculated.\n\nBut let me verify with a smaller example to ensure that this approach is correct.\n\nSuppose we have numbers from1 to3, each residue class mod3:1,2,3.\n\nResidues:\n\n1\u22611, 2\u22612,3\u22610.\n\nSo each residue has1 number. Now, how many combinations of three numbers where sum is divisible by3?\n\nBut we only have three numbers, so the only combination is1,2,3. Their sum is6, which is divisible by3. So one combination.\n\nAccording to our formula:\n\nNumber of all-same-residue combinations:3*C(1,3)=0 (since C(1,3)=0).\n\nNumber of one-from-each-residue:1*1*1=1.\n\nTotal combinations:0+1=1. Which matches.\n\nAnother example: numbers1-6.\n\nResidues:\n\n0:3,6 (2 numbers)\n\n1:1,4 (2 numbers)\n\n2:2,5 (2 numbers)\n\nNow, how many combinations of three numbers with sum divisible by3.\n\nTotal combinations C(6,3)=20.\n\nAccording to our approach:\n\nAll-same-residue: each residue has2 numbers, so C(2,3)=0 for each residue. Total3*0=0.\n\nOne-from-each-residue:2*2*2=8.\n\nBut wait, in this case, we can also have combinations like three numbers where two are from one residue and one from another. But according to the earlier logic, only all-same and one-from-each contribute to sum divisible by3.\n\nBut let's check manually:\n\nPossible combinations:\n\n1. 1,2,3: sum6\u21920\n\n2.1,2,6: sum9\u21920\n\n