, c) = b + c + 2023\\), follow this reasoning:\n\n1. **Key Observations**:\n   - Let \\(d_1 = \\operatorname{gcd}(a, b)\\), \\(d_2 = \\operatorname{gcd}(a, c)\\), \\(d_3 = \\operatorname{gcd}(b, c)\\).\n   - Each \\(\\operatorname{gcd}\\) term is at most the smaller of its two arguments. Thus:\n     - \\(d_1 \\leq b\\), \\(d_2 \\leq c\\), and \\(d_3 \\leq \\min(b, c)\\).\n   - The given equation implies:\n     \\[\n     d_1 + d_2 + d_3 = b + c + 2023.\n     \\]\n\n2. **Bounding \\(\\boldsymbol{d_3}\\)**:\n   - Since \\(d_1 \\leq b\\) and \\(d_2 \\leq c\\), substituting maxima gives:\n     \\[\n     b + c + d_3 \\geq b + c + 2023 \\implies d_3 \\geq 2023.\n     \\]\n   - Additionally, \\(d_3 \\leq \\min(b, c)\\). Thus, \\(\\min(b, c) \\geq 2023\\).\n\n3. **Structure of \\(\\boldsymbol{d_3}\\)**:\n   - Let \\(d_3 = 2023k\\) where \\(k \\geq 1\\). Then \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) are multiples of \\(2023k\\):\n     \\[\n     b = 2023k \\cdot m, \\quad c = 2023k \\cdot n, \\quad \\text{with } \\operatorname{gcd}(m, n) = 1.\n     \\]\n\n4. **Substitute into the Equation**:\n   - Substitute \\(b\\) and \\(c\\) into the original equation:\n     \\[\n     \\operatorname{gcd}(a, 2023k \\cdot m) + \\operatorname{gcd}(a, 2023k \\cdot n) + 2023k = 2023k(m + n) + 2023.\n     \\]\n   - Divide by \\(2023\\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{\\operatorname{gcd}(a, 2023k \\cdot m) + \\operatorname{gcd}(a, 2023k \\cdot n)}{2023} + k = k(m + n) + 1.\n     \\]\n\n5. **Analyzing \\(\\boldsymbol{k \\geq 2}\\)**:\n   - Assume \\(a = 2023k \\cdot p\\). Then:\n     \\[\n     \\operatorname{gcd}(2023k \\cdot p, 2023k \\cdot m) = 2023k \\cdot \\operatorname{gcd}(p, m),\n     \\]\n     and similarly for \\(n\\). Substituting back:\n     \\[\n     k(\\operatorname{gcd}(p, m) + \\operatorname{gcd}(p, n) + 1) = k(m + n) + 1.\n     \\]\n   - Simplify:\n     \\[\n     \\operatorname{gcd}(p, m) + \\operatorname{gcd}(p, n) + 1 = m + n + \\frac{1}{k}.\n     \\]\n   - For \\(k \\geq 2\\), \\(\\frac{1}{k}\\) is not an integer, leading to a contradiction.\n\n6. **Conclusion**:\n   - Hence, \\(k = 1\\) is the only valid possibility, implying:\n     \\[\n     \\operatorname{gcd}(b, c) = 2023.\n     \\]\n\n**Final Answer:** \\(\\boxed{2023}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe need to prove that \\(\\operatorname{gcd}(b, c) = 2023\\). Given the equation:\n\n\\[ \\operatorname{gcd}(a, b) + \\operatorname{gcd}(a, c) + \\operatorname{gcd}(b, c) = b + c + 2023, \\]\n\nwe first note that \\(\\operatorname{gcd}(a, b)\\), \\(\\operatorname{gcd}(a, c)\\), and \\(\\operatorname{gcd}(b, c)\\) are all divisors of \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) respectively. Let's denote:\n\n\\[ \\operatorname{gcd}(a, b) = d_1, \\quad \\operatorname{gcd}(a, c) = d_2, \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\operatorname{gcd}(b, c) = d_3. \\]\n\nWe substitute these into the equation:\n\n\\[ d_1 + d_2 + d_3 = b + c + 2023. \\]\n\n### Case Analysis:\n\n**Case I: Both \\(d_1\\) and \\(d_2\\) are not maximal divisors of \\(b\\) and \\(c\\)**\n\nHere, we assume \\(d_1 \\leq \\frac{b}{u}\\) and \\(d_2 \\leq \\frac{c}{v}\\) for some \\(u > 1\\) and \\(v > 1\\):\n\n\\[ \\frac{b}{u} + \\frac{c}{v} + d_3 \\geq b + c + 2023, \\]\n\nwhich implies:\n\n\\[ d_3 \\geq b + c + 2023 - \\frac{b}{u} - \\frac{c}{v}. \\]\n\nGiven \\(u, v > 1\\):\n\n\\[ \\frac{b}{u} < b \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{c}{v} < c, \\]\n\nso:\n\n\\[ d_3 > \\frac{b + c}{2}, \\]\n\nhowever, this contradicts the fact that \\(d_3 = \\operatorname{gcd}(b, c)\\) is a divisor and cannot exceed \\(b\\) or \\(c\\), thus cannot exceed \\(\\frac{b + c}{2}\\).\n\n**Conclusion**:   \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) must equal 1 for no contradiction.\n\n### Verification:\nGiven now \\(\\operatorname{gcd}(a, b) = b\\) and \\(\\operatorname{gcd}(a, c) = c\\):\n\n\\[ b + c + d_3 = b + c + 2023, \\]\n\nhence, \\(d_3 = 2023\\), and thus:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\operatorname{gcd}(b, c) = 2023}. \\", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "From point \\(