te to the area of the triangle. Wait, there's something called Viviani's theorem, which states that in an equilateral triangle, the sum of the distances from any interior point to the sides is equal to the height of the triangle. But this triangle isn't necessarily equilateral, so Viviani's theorem might not apply directly here. Hmm.\n\nBut maybe we can use the areas of smaller triangles created by point M. If we draw perpendiculars from M to each side, then the area of triangle ABC can be expressed as the sum of the areas of triangles MBC, MAC, and MAB. Let me verify that.\n\nThe area of triangle ABC would be equal to the sum of the areas of triangles MBC, MAC, and MAB. Each of these smaller triangles has a height equal to the perpendiculars k, l, and m respectively. So, Area of ABC = (1/2)*BC*k + (1/2)*AC*l + (1/2)*AB*m. That seems correct. Let me write that formula:\n\nArea ABC = (1/2)*BC*k + (1/2)*AC*l + (1/2)*AB*m.\n\nBut wait, we don't know the lengths of BC, AC, and AB. We know the angles at A and B, but not the sides. So perhaps we need to express the sides in terms of each other using the Law of Sines or Law of Cosines?\n\nGiven angles at A and B, we can find angle at C. Since the sum of angles in a triangle is \u03c0, angle C = \u03c0 - \u03b1 - \u03b2 = \u03c0 - \u03c0/6 - \u03c0/4. Let's compute that:\n\n\u03c0 is 12/12 \u03c0, so \u03c0/6 is 2/12 \u03c0, \u03c0/4 is 3/12 \u03c0. So angle C = 12/12 \u03c0 - 2/12 \u03c0 - 3/12 \u03c0 = 7/12 \u03c0. So angle C is 7\u03c0/12. Okay.\n\nNow, using the Law of Sines, we can relate the sides. Let me denote the sides opposite angles A, B, C as a, b, c respectively. Wait, standard notation is side a is opposite angle A, side b opposite angle B, side c opposite angle C. So in this case, angle A is \u03c0/6, so side a is BC, angle B is \u03c0/4, side b is AC, angle C is 7\u03c0/12, side c is AB.\n\nWait, maybe I should clarify:\n\nIn triangle ABC, angle at A is \u03b1 = \u03c0/6, so side BC is opposite angle A, so s