.\n\n3. **Contradictions with Multiple Non-Integers**:\n   - If there are two non-integers \\( x = \\frac{a}{b} \\) and \\( y = \\frac{c}{d} \\), their product \\( xy \\) must be a perfect square. This leads to complex constraints that are impossible to satisfy unless \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) are integers.\n   - Introducing a third number \\( z \\) leads to further contradictions, as shown through detailed analysis of arithmetic and geometric mean conditions.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe necessity for each number to satisfy the condition with every other number, including itself, forces all numbers to be integers. Thus, we conclude:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All the given numbers are integers.}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "1. **Initialization**:\n   We will begin by verifying the property for \\( n = 2 \\).\n   Consider two strictly positive integers \\( a_1 \\) and \\( a_2 \\).\n   - If their arithmetic mean is an integer, then:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{a_1 + a_2}{2} \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n     \\]\n   - If their geometric mean is an integer, then:\n     \\[\n     \\sqrt{a_1 a_2} \\in \\mathbb{Z}\n     \\]\n   In both cases, the two integers must be such that the conditions for their respective means being integers hold true.\n\n2. **Induction Hypothesis**:\n   Assume the property holds for \\( n = k \\) integers; that is, for any \\( k \\) integers \\( a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_k \\):\n   - Either the arithmetic mean of every pair of these integers is an integer,\n   - Or the geometric mean of every pair of these integers is an integer.\n   \n3. **Induction Step**:\n   We need to show that the property holds for \\( k + 1 \\) integers \\( a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_k, a_{k+1} \\).\n\n   - Suppose all arithmetic means among the \\( k \\) integers \\( a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_k \\) are integers. We need to extend this property to include \\( a_{k+1} \\).\n   - Consider the arithmetic mean with the new integer \\( a_{k+1} \\):\n     \\[\n     \\frac{a_\\ell + a_{k+1}}{2} \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\qu