he other is odd, both (k - l) and (k + l) are odd. Therefore, depending on the parity of a, 101a is either even or odd, and the factors (k - l) and (k + l) must both be even or both be odd.\n\nBut since 101 is odd, 101a is odd if a is odd and even if a is even. Therefore, if a is even, 101a is even, so (k - l) and (k + l) are both even. If a is odd, 101a is odd, so (k - l) and (k + l) are both odd.\n\nSo, considering that 101a = (k - l)(k + l). Since 101 is prime, the factors must be either (1, 101a) or (101, a). However, since (k + l) > (k - l), and both factors must multiply to 101a. Let's analyze these two cases:\n\nCase 1: (k - l) = 1 and (k + l) = 101a\n\nCase 2: (k - l) = 101 and (k + l) = a\n\nBut wait, in case 2, since (k - l) = 101 and (k + l) = a. However, since a is a two-digit number (at least 10), but (k + l) = a, which is at least 10. But (k - l) = 101. Then, solving these equations:\n\nk - l = 101\n\nk + l = a\n\nAdding the two equations: 2k = 101 + a => k = (101 + a)/2\n\nSubtracting: 2l = a - 101 => l = (a - 101)/2\n\nBut l must be a positive integer. Since a is at least 10, (a - 101)/2 would be negative, which is impossible because l is a positive integer. Therefore, this case is impossible. Therefore, only Case 1 is possible.\n\nTherefore, only Case 1: (k - l) = 1 and (k + l) = 101a. Then, solving these two equations:\n\nAdding the equations:\n\n2k = 1 + 101a => k = (1 + 101a)/2\n\nSubtracting the equations:\n\n2l = 101a - 1 => l = (101a - 1)/2\n\nSince k and l must be integers, (1 + 101a) must be even, so 101a must be odd, which implies that a must be odd. Because 101 is odd, odd*odd = odd, odd*even = even. Therefore, in this case, a must be odd. So, a is an odd two-digit number between 10 and 49. So a \u2208 {11,13,...,49}.\n\nTherefore, we can express k and l in terms of a, and since N = l\u00b2 and M = k\u00b2, we can also write:\n\nN = l\u00b2 = [(101a -1)/2]^2\n\nM = k\u00b2 = [(101a +1)/2]^2\n\nBut also, N = 100a + b. Therefore,\n\n[(101a -