sible. Using the averaging argument, the critical threshold is $f \\leq \\frac{2}{3}$. For $f = \\frac{3}{4} > \\frac{2}{3}$, the required correct answers from good students exceed the maximum possible due to structural constraints, leading to a contradiction.\n\nc) **No**, similarly, replacing $2/3$ with $7/10$ (which also exceeds $\\frac{2}{3}$) results in impossibility. Despite total correct answers appearing sufficient, distributing them to meet both the problem difficulty and student performance constraints fails due to overlapping limitations.\n\n### Final Answers:\na) $\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}$  \nb) $\\boxed{\\text{No}}$  \nc) $\\boxed{\\text{No}}$", "ground_truth_solution": "\nTo address the problem, we need to evaluate the different parts (a, b, c) to determine the feasibility given the different conditions.\n\n#### Part (a) \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\) \n\nConsider:\n- \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\) of the problems were considered hard.\n- Each hard problem was not solved by at least \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\) of the students.\n- At least \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\) of the students wrote the control test well, solving at least \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\) of all problems.\n\nLet's illustrate with an example:\n- Suppose there are 3 problems: \\( A, B, C \\).\n- Let one-third of the students solve problems \\( A \\) and \\( C \\).\n- Another third solve \\( B \\) and \\( C \\).\n- The remaining third solve none of the problems.\n\nThis satisfies the conditions:\n- \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) are hard problems (each solved by \\( \\frac{1}{3} \\)) and not solved by \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\), meeting the hard problems' criteria.\n- Problem \\( C \\) is easy, solved by \\( \\frac{2}{3} \\).\n\nThus, it is possible for the conditions to be met.\n\n#### Part (b) \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\)\n\nWe will use a unit square to represent the problem. Let's draw a table where:\n- Rows represent students.\n- Columns represent problems.\n- If a student solves a problem, the corresponding intersection cell is marked.\n\n### Evaluation Method:\n1. Identify the area of the black regions in the grid using:\n   - At least \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\) of rows represent students writing well.\n   - At least \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\) columns represent difficult problems.\n\n2. Calculate the area:\n   - Top rows by good students have at least \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\times \\frac{3}{4} = 9/16 \\).\n   \n   - In left columns (difficult problems), less than \\( \\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{3}{4} = 3/16 \\), plus at most \\( \\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{4} = 1/16 \\) in remaining columns, totaling \\( \\le 7/16 \\).\n\nThis contradiction (\\(9/16 \\ge 7/16\\)) assumes the impossibility.\n\n#### Part (c) \\( \\frac{7}{10} \\)\n\nUse similar evaluations with a unit square where:\n- Rows represent good students \\(0.7\\), \n- Columns hard \\(0.7\\).\n\nCalculate:\n1. Total potential overlap area: \\( 0.7 \\times 0.7 = 0.49 \\).\n\n2. Evaluate with different constraints:\n   - \\( 0.7 \\times 0.3 = 0.21 \\) aggregate height in one scenario,\n   - \\( 0.3 \\times 0.7 = 0.21 \\) in another.\n\nSince by evaluating in both ways, the max area \\( 0.3 \\times 0.7 = 0.21\\) contradicts with \\(0.7 \\times 0.7 = 0.49\\).\n\n### Conclusion:\n\n- (a) It is possible:\n  \\(\\boxed{\\text{a. Yes}}\\).\n- (b) Not possible; replace \\( \\frac{2}{3}\\) with \\( \\frac{3}{4} \\):\n  \\(\\boxed{\\text{b. No}}\\).\n- (c) Not possible; replace \\( \\frac{2}{3}\\) with \\( \\frac{7}{10}\\):\n  \\(\\boxed{\\text{c. No}}\\).", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let \\( k \\) be a positive integer. A positive integer \\( n \\) is said to be a \\( k \\)-flip if the digits of \\( n \\) are reversed in order when it is multiplied by \\( k \\). For example, 1089 is a 9-flip because \\( 1089 \\times 9 = 9801 \\), and 21978 is a 4-flip because \\( 21978 \\times 4 = 87912 \\). Explain why there is no 7-flip integer.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out why there's no such 