ors) conflicts with LCM properties. For example, three LCMs \\( m-d \\), \\( m \\), \\( m+d \\) would require \\( m = \\frac{(m-d) + (m+d)}{2} \\), but LCMs rarely satisfy such additive relationships due to their multiplicative nature.\n\n4. **Scalability Issues**:\n   - As \\( n \\) increases, the number of pairs grows quadratically. Maintaining a constant difference across thousands of LCMs is infeasible due to the exponential growth potential of LCMs (e.g., LCM of large numbers can dwarf smaller LCMs, disrupting the AP).\n\n**Conclusion**: The inherent multiplicative nature of LCMs and the strict additive requirement of APs are irreconcilable, especially for \\( n > 100 \\). Thus, it is impossible for the LCMs of \\( n \\) distinct natural numbers to form a non-constant arithmetic progression when \\( n > 100 \\).\n\n\\(\\boxed{\\text{No}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Let us define the numbers written by Vitya in the notebook as a set of integers \\(\\{a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n\\}\\).\n\n2. For each pair of numbers in this set, Vitya wrote the least common multiple (LCM) on the board. Therefore, there are \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) such LCMs written on the board.\n\n3. We need to determine whether it is possible for these \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) numbers to form a sequence that is an arithmetic progression with a non-constant common difference for some \\(n>100\\).\n\n4. Assume that such an \\(n > 100\\) exists, and consider the \"most beautiful set\" of \\(n\\) numbers, i.e., the set that yields the sum of LCMs with the smallest possible total.\n\n5. If the common difference \\(d\\) of the arithmetic progression of the LCMs is such that \\(d>0\\) and \\(d\\) has a common prime factor \\(p\\) with any element in the arithmetic progression, then all members of that progression must be divisible by \\(p\\).\n\n6. Consider a similar argument where the set \\(\\{a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_n\\}\\) is divided by some prime \\(p\\):\n\n    - Each number in the set, except pos